{"title":"Bertrand's Postulate","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Formulation\">Formulation</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Bertrand%27s_Postulate&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Formulation\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><b>Bertrand's postulate</b> states that for any <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Positive_integer\" title=\"Positive integer\">positive integer</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, there is a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime_number\" title=\"Prime number\">prime</a> between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/0/d7005cc2dc7f084de8debbd99ce593b6a7730acd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n-2$\" width=\"50\" height=\"12\" />.  Despite its name, it is, in fact, a theorem. A more widely known version states that there is a prime between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/3/f93f5c51ea4b04b4992b003b39479f29018f6bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Bertrand%27s_Postulate&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>It is similar to the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chebyshev%27s_estimate_for_the_number_of_primes\" title=\"Chebyshev&#39;s estimate for the number of primes\">proof of Chebyshev's estimates</a> in the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Prime_Number_Theorem\" title=\"Prime Number Theorem\">prime number theorem</a> article but requires a closer look at the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Combinations\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Combinations\">binomial coefficient</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/a/6fa6acc025777f3be7226cb7e1caa698d6612940.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{2n}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"44\" height=\"53\" />. Assuming that the reader is familiar with that proof, the Bertrand postulate can be proved as follows.\n</p><p>Note that the power with which a prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> satisfying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/0/e90ccf858f7d88a4566094e5a4cac7dff0676cb0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2n}3&lt;p\\le n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"92\" height=\"37\" /> appears in the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/a/6fa6acc025777f3be7226cb7e1caa698d6612940.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{2n}{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"44\" height=\"53\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/9/5299a6f4e6f987ee013bc96e66748d544678486d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left\\lfloor\\frac{2n}{p}\\right\\rfloor- 2\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{p}\\right\\rfloor=2-2=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"212\" height=\"43\" />. Thus,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/1/2916a5060d31f76fd11adf3db5ab2a2a024bf154.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2^{2n}}{(2n+1)}\\le{\\binom{2n}{n}}\\le \\left(\\prod_{p\\le\\sqrt{2n}}p^{\\lfloor\\log_p (2n)\\rfloor}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\prod_{\\sqrt{2n}&lt;p\\le\\frac{2n}3}p\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\prod_{n&lt;p\\le {2n}}p\\right)\\,.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -27px\" width=\"555\" height=\"64\" />.\n</p><p>The first product does not exceed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/9/b7962b53748080e7c46bc77289433ce94bd6bc0b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2n)^{\\sqrt{2n}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"22\" /> and the second one does not exceed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/2/de2729f29b2c4eba587d3b59bed2b3c48df5eff6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4^{\\frac {2n}3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"18\" />. Thus,\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/c/bdcac573ceb5f2686ca5e43ae901d882cc51f730.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\prod_{n&lt;p\\le{2n}}p\\right)\\ge \\frac{4^{\\frac n3}}{(2n+1)(2n)^{\\sqrt {2n}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"243\" height=\"54\" />\n</p><p>The right hand side is strictly greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/6/996db8f4498d20629dfcd2540d3f581ea2dd421f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\ge 500$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"61\" height=\"15\" />, so it remains to prove the Bertrand postulate for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/3/643fedfa088774ef23ec3f5abbf917bc17382c34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&lt;500$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"61\" height=\"13\" />. In order to do it, it suffices to present a sequence of primes starting with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in which each prime does not exceed twice the previous one, and the last prime is above <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/9/a39b14023c98c943554d150561aacc188c4ce26d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$500$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />. One such possible sequence is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/8/cd865ef049a78b24239f3d3acfe1d2e4a01521da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2,3,5,7,13,23,43,83,163,317,631$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"270\" height=\"16\" />. Another possible sequence is \n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Bertrand%27s_Postulate&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018074527\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.019 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.030 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 83/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 309/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 3862/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.847      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.847      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:2251:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018074527 and revision id 239611. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}